result.rust_guidance:
USE f32 EVERYWHERE. The ONLY f64 in the whole port is the widening at print time (`v as f64`), mirroring the six `cvtss2sd` before `_M_insert<double>`. There is no other cast point.

Types:
```rust
#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
struct Rgb { r: f32, g: f32, b: f32 }   // matches the 12-byte, 3x4-byte C++ layout
```

Constants — write them as f32 literals; rustc encodes them to exactly the bit patterns in .rodata (verified):
```rust
const KR: f32 = 0.2126;  // 0x3E59B3D0
const KG: f32 = 0.7152;  // 0x3F371759
const KB: f32 = 0.0722;  // 0x3D93DD98
const ONE: f32 = 1.0;    // 0x3F800000
```

--- If you do NOT need bit-exact NaN signs, this is enough (matched 2985/2985 finite random cases) ---
```rust
fn invert(c: Rgb) -> Rgb { Rgb { r: 1.0f32 - c.r, g: 1.0f32 - c.g, b: 1.0f32 - c.b } }
fn luminance(c: Rgb) -> f32 { c.r * KR + c.g * KG + c.b * KB }   // Rust `+` is left-assoc == the asm
fn grayscale(c: Rgb) -> Rgb { let l = luminance(c); Rgb { r: l, g: l, b: l } }
```
Do NOT write `mul_add`, do NOT enable any fast-math, and do NOT reorder the sum. Rust never contracts to FMA without explicit `mul_add`, so plain `+`/`*` is safe. Keep `luminance` returning f32 and replicate that ONE value into all three components (never recompute per channel).

--- RECOMMENDED: bit-exact including NaN sign (the naive form is provably wrong for `inf -inf nan`) ---
Reproduce the x86 SSE rule "if the destination operand is NaN the result is the destination, else if the source is NaN the result is the source, else if the op is invalid the result is the real-indefinite 0xFFC00000":
```rust
const IND: u32 = 0xFFC0_0000;                 // x86 "real indefinite" QNaN, sign bit SET

/// mulss(v, k) where k is a finite non-zero constant -> can never create a NaN itself.
#[inline]
fn mul_k(v: f32, k: f32) -> f32 { if v.is_nan() { v } else { v * k } }

/// addss(x, y) with x as the DESTINATION operand: x's NaN wins over y's.
#[inline]
fn add_ss(x: f32, y: f32) -> f32 {
    if x.is_nan() { x }
    else if y.is_nan() { y }
    else { let s = x + y; if s.is_nan() { f32::from_bits(IND) } else { s } }  // inf + -inf
}

/// subss(1.0f, c): 1.0f is the destination and is never NaN; 1.0 - +/-inf is never invalid.
#[inline]
fn one_minus(c: f32) -> f32 { if c.is_nan() { c } else { 1.0f32 - c } }

fn luminance(c: Rgb) -> f32 {
    let t1 = mul_k(c.r, KR);        // mulss xmm1(r), KR
    let t2 = mul_k(c.g, KG);        // mulss xmm0(KG), g   -- KG never NaN, so order is moot
    let t3 = add_ss(t1, t2);        // addss xmm1(t1), t2  -- t1 (i.e. r) wins over t2 (g)
    let t4 = mul_k(c.b, KB);        // mulss xmm0(KB), b
    add_ss(t4, t3)                  // addss xmm0(t4), t3  -- t4 (i.e. b) wins over t3
}

fn invert(c: Rgb) -> Rgb { Rgb { r: one_minus(c.r), g: one_minus(c.g), b: one_minus(c.b) } }
fn grayscale(c: Rgb) -> Rgb { let l = luminance(c); Rgb { r: l, g: l, b: l } }
```
Note the NaN priority this yields, which is what the binary does: b's NaN beats r's NaN beats g's NaN beats the indefinite 0xFFC00000. That is exactly why `inf -inf nan` prints `nan` (b's +NaN overrides the -NaN produced by inf + -inf) while `inf -inf 1` prints `-nan`.

Do NOT add any clamping (`.clamp()`, `min`, `max`) anywhere — maxss/minss appear 0 times in the binary and clamp<float> is unreachable from this path. Negative and >1 results must flow through untouched.

Signed zero: `1.0f32 - (-0.0f32) == 1.0` and `(-0.0)*K + (-0.0)*K + (-0.0)*K == -0.0`; plain f32 ops already give this, and your formatter must print `-0` (not `0`) for a negative zero.

Parsing: use `s.parse::<f32>()` (correctly rounded to f32, same as strtof — no double intermediate). Then, to match `std::stof`, reject non-zero subnormals: after a successful parse, if `v != 0.0 && v.abs() < f32::MIN_POSITIVE` (1.1754944e-38) treat it as `std::out_of_range`. Also reject anything that overflows to infinity when the input text was not literally inf/nan. (Hand this to whoever owns the parsing topic.)

Printing: `v as f64` then %g-with-precision-6. That single `as f64` is the ONLY widening in the program.

Everything below was validated byte-for-byte against the reference binary on a 2914-case corpus plus targeted edge probes. Dependency-free, no unsafe needed.

1) FORMATTING — this exact `g6` matched glibc `%g` (precision 6) on 2914/2914 cases including round-half-even ties, exponent bumps, subnormals, -0, inf and nan:

```rust
fn g6(x: f32) -> String {
    if x.is_nan()      { return if x.is_sign_negative() { "-nan".into() } else { "nan".into() }; }
    if x.is_infinite() { return if x < 0.0 { "-inf".into() } else { "inf".into() }; }
    if x == 0.0        { return if x.is_sign_negative() { "-0".into() } else { "0".into() }; }
    let e = format!("{:.5e}", x);                       // Rust rounds half-to-even, same as glibc
    let (mant, es) = e.split_once('e').unwrap();
    let exp: i32 = es.parse().unwrap();                 // exponent AFTER rounding — matches C99 %g rule
    if exp < -4 || exp >= 6 {
        let mut m = mant.to_string();
        if m.contains('.') { while m.ends_with('0') { m.pop(); } if m.ends_with('.') { m.pop(); } }
        format!("{}e{}{:02}", m, if exp < 0 { '-' } else { '+' }, exp.abs())   // >=2 exponent digits
    } else {
        let mut s = format!("{:.*}", (5 - exp) as usize, x);
        if s.contains('.') { while s.ends_with('0') { s.pop(); } if s.ends_with('.') { s.pop(); } }
        s
    }
}
```
Do NOT use `{}`/Display for the floats: Rust's shortest-round-trip prints `0.578860` differently (e.g. `1e30` vs `1e+30`, `0.2126` ok but `1.0e-7` etc. diverge) and never emits `-nan`/`inf` in C form.

2) ARITHMETIC — pure f32, left-associative, with explicit NaN-winner selection (a plain `(a+b)+c` mismatches the binary on -nan cases; rustc also const-folds NaN to +NaN):

```rust
const CR: f32 = 0.2126;  // 0x3E59B3D0
const CG: f32 = 0.7152;  // 0x3F371759
const CB: f32 = 0.0722;  // 0x3D93DD98
const NEG_QNAN: f32 = unsafe_free_from_bits(); // f32::from_bits(0xffc0_0000)

fn add_like_cpp(a: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {       // b is the ADDSS dest in the emitted code
    if b.is_nan() { return b; }
    if a.is_nan() { return a; }
    if a.is_infinite() && b.is_infinite() && a.is_sign_negative() != b.is_sign_negative() {
        return f32::from_bits(0xffc0_0000);    // x86 default NaN prints "-nan"
    }
    a + b
}
fn luma(r: f32, g: f32, b: f32) -> f32 {
    let t1 = if r.is_nan() { r } else { CR * r };   // mul preserves the NaN operand's sign
    let t2 = if g.is_nan() { g } else { CG * g };
    let t3 = if b.is_nan() { b } else { CB * b };
    // s12: t1 wins over t2 (t1 is the ADDSS dest here)
    let s12 = if t1.is_nan() { t1 } else { add_like_cpp(t2, t1) };
    // final: t3 wins over s12
    add_like_cpp(s12, t3)
}
fn invert(c: f32) -> f32 { if c.is_nan() { c } else { 1.0f32 - c } }
```
Value-wise `a+b == b+a` bitwise in IEEE, so this reproduces the left-associative value exactly; only the NaN sign is steered.

3) PARSING (`std::stof` == C `strtof` + errno check). Implement a strtof-shaped scanner:
 - skip leading `isspace` bytes (space, \t, \n, \v, \f, \r);
 - optional `+`/`-`;
 - then one of: `inf`/`infinity` (case-insensitive), `nan` with optional `(chars)` payload, `0x`/`0X` hex-float with optional `p` exponent, or decimal digits with optional `.` and `e`/`E` exponent;
 - trailing characters are IGNORED (no throw);
 - if nothing convertible was consumed -> print the invalid_argument abort message and abort.
 For the decimal value, `f32::from_str` on the consumed prefix is correctly rounded and matches strtof. Hex floats must be assembled manually (Rust has no hex-float parser).

4) RANGE THROWS (this is the biggest surprise — do not skip it). After determining the exact numeric value, throw `std::out_of_range` when:
```rust
const T_UNDER: f64 = 1.175494315789825899848309764129e-38; // 2f64.powi(-126) - 2f64.powi(-151)
const T_OVER : f64 = 3.402823567797336616375393954581e38;  // 2f64.powi(128)  - 2f64.powi(103)
// v64 = the parsed magnitude as f64 (or track exponent separately if it underflows f64 to 0 / overflows to inf)
let tiny = v64 != 0.0 && v64.abs() < T_UNDER;      // strict <  : the tie is NOT tiny (ties-to-even -> 2^-126)
let big  = v64.abs() >= T_OVER;                    // >=       : the tie IS an overflow
```
`inf`/`nan` literals are exempt. If the decimal underflows f64 to 0.0 but has nonzero significant digits, treat as tiny; if it overflows f64 to inf, treat as big. Compute these on the decimal, not on the f32 result.

5) ABORT PATH — write to stderr then die by SIGABRT (shell exit 134). Two spaces after `what():`:
```rust
fn cpp_terminate(kind: &str) -> ! {
    eprint!("terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::{}'\n  what():  stof\n", kind);
    std::process::abort();      // raises SIGABRT -> 134
}
```
Nothing must have been printed to stdout at that point (the throw happens before any output).

6) MAIN:
```rust
let a: Vec<String> = std::env::args().collect();
let (mut r, mut g, mut b) = (0.3f32, 0.7f32, 0.2f32);
if a.len() > 3 { r = stof(&a[1]); g = stof(&a[2]); b = stof(&a[3]); }  // strictly argc>3; extra args ignored
let l = luma(r, g, b);
println!("{} {} {} {} {} {}", g6(invert(r)), g6(invert(g)), g6(invert(b)), g6(l), g6(l), g6(l));
```
Single spaces, one `\n`, nothing else, exit 0. Do not touch locale, do not clamp, do not use f64 or `mul_add` in `luma`, and do not special-case negative zero (f32 arithmetic already yields `-0` for `-0 -0 -0` and `0` for `-0 0 0`).
